  "metadata": {
    "title": "Equivalent Workers: Convert Before You Chain",
    "description": "Convert unequal worker types into one common efficiency unit before using chain rule.",
    "teachingApproach": "Treat efficiency like currency exchange. First convert men, women, boys or machine types into one common work unit; only then use workers × hours × days. Begin with one equivalence, move to mixed groups, earnings and different work rates, then derive an equivalence from two completed-job statements. Finish with a mixed-group capstone involving changed hours and work amount.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 32,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 15, Chain Rule. Approved scope: Teach mixed-efficiency questions by converting every worker type to one common unit before using time or work ratios. Cover statements such as '5 men = 9 women', one worker doing twice another's work, men/women/boys together, earnings by mixed groups, and machine/engine types with different consumption or work rates. Include one case where an equivalence must be deduced from two completed-work statements. The central habit is 'convert first, chain second'."
  },
